{"author":"Rebar Bot","author_email":"joeoakhart+bot@navapbc.com","author_id":"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d","author_sig":"{\"payload\": \"\", \"payloadType\": \"application/vnd.rebar.authorship.v1+json\", \"signatures\": [{\"keyid\": \"594c-9dcf-5ad6-4e6d\", \"sig\": \"\"}]}","data":{"body":"## Root cause CONFIRMED by per-call measurement on i-00880b2c7f13527c5\n\nMeasured each suspect call individually on the production host (bounded, read-only, SSM).\nWall-clock per call:\n\n| call | measured | bound in probe | completes? |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `du -sx --block-size=1 /var/lib/docker` | **124.18s** (at a 130s probe bound) | `DOCKER_DU_TIMEOUT=120` | **NO** — always hits the cap |\n| `du -sx --block-size=1 /var/lib/docker/overlay2` | same tree, second walk | `DOCKER_DU_TIMEOUT=120` | **NO** |\n| `docker system df` | **25.01s** (at a 25s bound) | `DOCKER_DF_TIMEOUT=20` | **NO** |\n| `journalctl -u rebar-autodeploy.service -o cat \\| grep -c` | 8.19–14.11s | **unbounded** | yes |\n| `journalctl CONTAINER_NAME=compose-review-bot-1 …` | 4.41s | **unbounded** | yes |\n| `journalctl CONTAINER_NAME=compose-gerrit-1 …` | 8.93s | **unbounded** | yes |\n| `du -sx /var/log/journal` | 0.01s | 60s | yes |\n| `docker ps` / `docker stats --no-stream` | 0.04s / 2.62s | 15s | yes |\n| gerrit/review-bot/MCP curl probes | 0.10s | `--max-time 10` | yes |\n\n### The lead HELD, and it is worse than one `du`\n\n`docker_du_bytes` is called **TWICE** (observability.sh:592-593) — once over `$DOCKER_ROOT` and\nagain over `$DOCKER_ROOT/overlay2`, which is essentially the same ~1.88M-file tree walked a\nsecond time. Each is bounded at `DOCKER_DU_TIMEOUT=120` and each **always hits that bound**:\n\n    592: docker_total_bytes=\"$(docker_du_bytes \"$DOCKER_ROOT\")\"            -> 120s, returns nothing\n    593: docker_overlay2_bytes=\"$(docker_du_bytes \"$DOCKER_ROOT/overlay2\")\" -> 120s, returns nothing\n\n**2 x 120s = 240s = TimeoutStartSec exactly.** The probe is SIGTERM-ed inside or immediately\nafter the second `du`. That is the whole 4-minute wall clock, and because `du` blocks on I/O\nrather than computing, it contributes almost nothing to the ~35s CPU — which is the\nwall-vs-CPU gap in the report.\n\nBeing *bounded* is not being *cheap*: the existing `timeout 120` guard converted an unbounded\nhang into a **guaranteed 120s of burnt budget that returns no value**. Twice.\n\n### Why it is this slow: the unit runs at idle I/O priority\n\n`install-observability.sh` sets `IOSchedulingClass=idle` (and `Nice=10`) on the unit. On a host\nwith documented IOPS-saturation history (`smudgeless-friendless-longspur`), a 1.88M-file walk\nat idle I/O priority is starved by design. So this is not fixable by raising the timeout or by\noptimising computation — both were correctly ruled out in the report.\n\n### Second contributor: 12 full-journal scans, all unbounded\n\nThe journal is **1.7 GB across 38 files**. The probe scans it end-to-end **12 times** in 5\ndistinct shapes — `publish_autodeploy_marker_delta` alone re-reads the identical\n`journalctl -u rebar-autodeploy.service` stream **8 times** (lines 1310, 1312, 1318, 1322, 1330,\n1343, 1348, 1350), plus one at 1301, plus voter/merge (2) and g2p (1). At the measured 8-14s per\nautodeploy scan that is **~110s**, and it is the bulk of the ~35s CPU (journalctl decode + grep).\n\nNeither contributor alone exceeds 240s. **Together they do**: 240 (du) + 20 (df) + ~110 (journal)\n= **~370s of work in a 240s budget.**\n\n### The cut point, and it matches the reported metric ages exactly\n\nMetrics publish in file order. The kill lands at line ~593:\n\n- line 97-237 — `gerrit_healthy`, `reviewbot_healthy`, `GerritReachable`, `mcp_healthy`,\n  `disk_used_percent`, **`root_disk_used_percent` (192)**, `gate_scratch_mounted` — **all publish**.\n- line 597+ — `docker_storage_bytes`, `journal_*`, `var_tmp_*`, `container_*`,\n  **`mem_used_percent` (1020)**, `container_memory_rss_bytes`, `voter_errors`, `deploy_errors`,\n  `g2p_dispatch_errors`, `mirror_out_of_sync` — **never reached**.\n\nThat is precisely the reported evidence: `root_disk_used_percent` fresh at 18:25Z,\n`mem_used_percent` stale. The cut point moves with I/O load, which is why some late metrics\nhave occasional old datapoints rather than none.\n\n### Confirmed still failing right now\n\nJournal, this hour — every run killed at exactly 4min:\n\n    18:20:49 Starting ... 18:24:49 killed 15/TERM, 'timeout', Consumed 39.687s CPU\n    18:25:50 Starting ... 18:29:50 killed 15/TERM, 'timeout', Consumed 34.131s CPU\n    18:31:13 Starting ... 18:35:13 killed 15/TERM, 'timeout', Consumed 39.518s CPU\n\n### One correction to the report\n\n`gerrit_healthy` is published EARLY (line 97) and does survive the kill, so \"no datapoints at\nall\" is not explained by truncation. It is published **with an `InstanceId` dimension** while its\nsibling `GerritReachable` is dimensionless; a query without that dimension returns nothing.\nThat is a query-side artifact, not a second defect in the probe.\n"},"env_id":"eea68b7f-6697-488b-ac52-744e2ed8e96f","event_type":"COMMENT","timestamp":1788633811584367013,"uuid":"ffd36ea6-2225-48f7-9780-a2ec8ec790fb"}